== Corporate affairs ==

=== Ownership ===

Aeroístmus is publicly traded on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] under the ticker symbol AER. The airline was privatised in the mid-1980s through an initial public offering that sold a majority stake to private investors. The ownership structure is as follows:

* [[Fons d'Inversió Directa de Balboa]] (FIDB): 8% — retained from the privatisation; strategic government interest
* [[Balboa Pension Fund]] (BPF): 6% — institutional anchor investor
* Public float: approximately 86% — held by institutional and retail investors

The Balboan government retains no direct equity stake but provides subsidies for the essential services programme through the Ministry of Transport.

=== Essential services programme ===

Aeroístmus operates a network of government-subsidised essential service routes to destinations within Balboa that would not be commercially viable without public support. The programme, funded through the Ministry of Transport, ensures connectivity to remote communities, islands, and low-population areas. Routes operated under the essential services programme include services to [[Illes del Guano Airport|Illes del Guano]], [[Marie Torres Airport|Sant Agneu]], and selected interior highland destinations. The programme is reviewed periodically by the Ministry, and routes are subject to competitive tender.

=== Frequent flyer program ===

{{main|Star Circle}}

[[Star Circle]] is the frequent flyer program of Aeroístmus, launched in 2008. The program uses a spend-based earning model, awarding members 5 [[Aero Points]] per $1 spent on base fares and carrier surcharges. Star Circle is integrated with the [[Adonian Arrow]] alliance, allowing members to earn and redeem points across all alliance partner flights. The program offers four elite tiers — Silver, Gold, Platinum, and Diamond — with benefits including priority check-in, lounge access, and cabin upgrades. A co-branded credit card is issued by [[Banc Balboa]] in three tiers.

=== In-house catering ===

<!-- Image: Top-down photo of an Aeroístmus business class meal on white porcelain with Aeroístmus logo. Balboan cuisine: grilled mahi-mahi with mango-lime sauce, coconut rice, fried plantain slices, pickled red onion, small dish of aji chombo hot sauce, side salad of tropical greens. Branded cutlery and linen napkin. -->
[[File:Aeroístmus business class meal.png|thumb|right|300px|A business class meal prepared by Aeroístmus's in-house catering division, featuring Balboan cuisine.]]

Aeroístmus operates an in-house catering division at both hubs, employing approximately 115 staff across production kitchens at SCI and SBT, loading and delivery teams, menu development, and quality control. The division showcases [[Balboan cuisine]] across all cabin classes, from economy to first class, and partners with restaurants in Sant Cristòfor — including [[Atsui (restaurant)|Atsui]] and [[DesprésDesprés (restaurant)|DesprésDesprés]] — on menu development and seasonal dishes.

== Cargo operations ==

Aeroístmus operates a dedicated cargo division, branded Aeroístmus Cargo, from its hub at [[Sant Cristòfor International Airport]]. The division operates one {{w|Boeing 777F}} freighter on scheduled cargo services between Sant Cristòfor and [[Ampuria International Airport|Ampuria]] in [[Volisania]], carrying high-value and time-sensitive goods between the two markets. In addition to the dedicated freighter, Aeroístmus Cargo utilises the belly cargo capacity of its passenger fleet across the entire route network, providing cargo services to all destinations served by the airline.

The principal cargo flows through Sant Cristòfor consist of Balboan exports — including seafood, tropical fruit, coffee, cocoa, textiles, and manufactured goods — and imports of machinery, electronics, and pharmaceuticals. The Volisania route handles the largest volume of freighter cargo, with the 777F operating the SCI–Ampuria sector multiple times weekly, supplementing the belly cargo capacity of the passenger {{w|Boeing 777|777-300ER}} services on the same route. Belly cargo is carried on all passenger services across the domestic and international network, with cargo handling facilities at both SCI and [[Cecilio Ríos International Airport|Sant Bart]].

